{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfqgQ74NCdpsr2XteRiLu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I returned all three rows, so the batch outcome is `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=3`. The verdict is committed as `2c5ba50b` with the `WI: WI-860` trailer, and the pre-commit checks passed. Since I approved nothing, no registry cell changed and I did not run the approval snapshot.\n\n**Why each row went back:**\n- **SR-233:**\n  - Its `Coincident` cell says SN-006 requires a run to avoid \"unbounded effort on conditions it cannot remedy\". SN-006 says nothing like that, and the row's own Rationale (correctly) calls it a derived requirement. The cell misstates the parent and contradicts the row.\n  - Its acceptance criterion binds only the reviewer brief. WI-860's Done-when binds every brief that judges a finding, so the brief WI-865 is about to add would carry no obligation from this row.\n  - It doesn't say who records the one-line dismissal or where, although the owner's ruling names both.\n- **LLR-311:** Its only parent is returned, and fixing that parent changes what this row must say. It also calls the definition's home a \"named heading\", but what was built is a bold lead-in paragraph.\n- **TC-332:** The test checks three things: the paragraph exists once, the reviewer brief cites it, and no prompt contains \"a fake or hostile binary\". It never checks what the definition says. Someone could delete \"never answered with code\", the one-line dismissal, or the in-scope list and the test would still pass. Its Expected cell also quietly narrows \"does not copy its boundary examples\" (there are four) down to one example.\n\n**The follow-up:** I drafted one follow-up item in the `## Dispositions` section of WI-860's spec, and the intake parser reads it cleanly. It covers re-texting the three rows, adding content checks to the test, and bringing all three back for first approval. It gets an ID when this lane merges.\n\n**Where this deviates from the brief:** the commit holds two files, not the \"ONE file\" the brief asked for. The return section requires the follow-up draft in the spec's `## Dispositions`, and the intake reads it from there. I committed both together, the way the WI-846 sitting did (`0f98ed06`). I left the coordinator's untracked `.requested` marker alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":883,"cache_read_input_tokens":93264,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":883},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8cf33a7-067d-4b49-9e30-523229883c86","uuid":"5a60737c-bfe4-4c20-b817-2fce7eff355d","timestamp":"2026-10-09T00:00:42.962Z","request_id":"req_011CfqgQ6nzsECFu83hR7gCx"}
